---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MachineTop from '../components/MachineTop.astro';
import SampleList from '../components/SampleList.astro';
import { bySlug } from '../data/machines';

const m = bySlug('ob-poly-xp');

// The strip under the header: the page's sections, the one being read underlined.
const sections = [
  { id: 'download', label: 'Download' },
  { id: 'hear', label: 'Hear it' },
  { id: 'accuracy', label: 'In numbers' },
  { id: 'differences', label: 'Differences' },
  { id: 'made', label: 'How it was made' },
  { id: 'rom', label: 'ROMs' },
  { id: 'artwork', label: 'Artwork' },
];

// The real units in the recordings, on YouTube.
const video = (id: string) => `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=${id}`;
---
<Base title="ob-poly-xp · Lemus" description="ob-poly-xp emulates the Oberheim Xpander: its original firmware on two emulated 6809s, and its six analog voices modelled part by part. Free for macOS and Windows." current="ob-poly-xp">

<nav slot="sub" class="onpage" aria-label="On this page">
<div class="frame sheet">
<div class="col">
<ol>
{sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`} class:list={{ red: s.id === 'differences' }}>{s.label}</a></li>)}
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</nav>

<MachineTop machine={m}>
<SampleList heading="Factory patches" plugin="ob-poly-xp" tracks={[
  { src: '/media/ob-poly-xp/strings.m4a', title: 'STRINGS1', detail: 'Patch 23, four chords' },
  { src: '/media/ob-poly-xp/apollo.m4a', title: 'APOLLO', detail: 'Patch 50, a bass line' },
  { src: '/media/ob-poly-xp/sgenviv.m4a', title: 'S.GENVIV', detail: 'Patch 06, a melody' },
  { src: '/media/ob-poly-xp/bamboo.m4a', title: 'BAMBOO', detail: 'Patch 46, 3 PHASE+1 LOW' },
]} />
<Fragment slot="data">
<div><dt class="lbl">ROM images</dt><dd>Six EPROMs, main software 1.2 and voice software 1.4, not included</dd></div>
</Fragment>
</MachineTop>

<section class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<figure class="panel">
<div class="mount">
<div class="pin">
<img src="/media/ob-poly-xp/panel.png" alt="ob-poly-xp: the Xpander's front panel with its three displays, the six encoders, the page and modulation keys, the keypad and VOLUME, factory patch 23 STRINGS1 on the display, its brand marks' places left blank, and the plugin bar with REAR and ?" width="1514" height="603">
<span class="leader" style="left: 46%; top: -24px; height: calc(42% + 24px)" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="target" style="left: 46%; top: 42%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="balloon" style="left: 46%; top: -56px" aria-hidden="true">A1</span>
<span class="leader" style="left: 80%; top: 30%; height: calc(70% + 24px)" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="target" style="left: 80%; top: 30%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="balloon" style="left: 80%; top: calc(100% + 24px)" aria-hidden="true">A2</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="caption">
<div class="row"><span class="key">A1</span><span><strong>Panel, patches and modulation.</strong> The main and voice processors, two 6809s, run Oberheim's firmware from the six EPROMs.</span></div>
<div class="row"><span class="key">A2</span><span><strong>Six voices.</strong> Two CEM3374 oscillators and a CEM3372 filter with its 15 modes each, modelled part by part from the service manual and the chips' data sheets.</span></div>
<div class="row"><span class="fig">FIG. 1</span><span>Factory patch 23 on the VCO1 page, the brand marks' places left blank, and the plugin bar with REAR and ?.</span></div>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>

<section id="accuracy" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<div class="numbers-head">
<h2 class="h2 tap">In numbers</h2>
<p class="dim">Checked against the service manual's own checks, an ngspice simulation of the transcribed circuit, freesound recordings of real Xpanders' notes and YouTube recordings of Xpanders and of Matrix-12s, which use the same voice boards. Method: <a href="#made">How it was made</a>.</p>
<p class="dim small">The clips at the top: ob-poly-xp alone, with no effects, playing Oberheim's factory patches (FACTORY1.SYX, which ob-poly-xp loads if it's in the ROM folder).</p>
</div>

<div class="numbers">
<div class="measure">
<div class="figure">
<div class="big">0.2 cents</div>
<div class="what">pitch per octave against real Xpanders, 33 Hz to 1 kHz</div>
<div class="dim small">Four sets of single notes from real units (freesound, CC0), after the firmware's own TUNE ALL. Note-to-note scatter 0.6 cents; the units', 0.5–1.0.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="measure">
<div class="figure">
<div class="big">1 dB</div>
<div class="what">the oscillators' harmonics against a Matrix-12, to the 19th</div>
<div class="dim small">The triangle and the pulse with the filter open, the triangle's even harmonics included; the noise generator within 1.5 dB from 25 Hz to 13 kHz; the 15 filter modes' shapes on a swept saw within 0.6–1.2 dB rms.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="measure">
<div class="figure">
<div class="big">9 / 9</div>
<div class="what">service-manual checks passed</div>
<div class="dim small">VERSION, MEM, VMEM, LEDS, DISP, the 4 V reference, the DAC's offset, DAC MONO, and TUNE ALL for VCOS, PW, RES, VCF and VCA, through the firmware. Against ngspice: 88 cases, the 16 pole mixes within 0.07 dB and the filter's oscillation within 0.02 cents.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="differences" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<h2 class="h2 tap red">What is still different</h2>
<dl class="diffs">
<div><dt>RES 62 to 63</dt><dd>With the filter cells' tanh, the oscillation at RES 63 sits about 265 cents under the cells' small-signal cutoff. The firmware tunes the filter on that oscillation, so from RES 62 to 63 the pitch drops about 2.6 semitones. Whether the real chip drops that far isn't known.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Fitted on one unit</dt><dd>VCF OUT's gain, 0.88 of the data sheet's, from one Matrix-12's phaser modes (a factory-demo patch agrees); the input VCAs' taper above 2.7 V, from two units, extrapolated below.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Chosen values</dt><dd>No recording pins the resonance VCA's CV law (linear), the output level (0 dBFS is 10 V at the outputs), or VOLUME's taper.</dd></div>
<div><dt>The voices are alike</dt><dd>Their levels differ as on one real unit, but their oscillators and filters have no spread, so one factory tune serves all six.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Units differ</dt><dd>The recordings at hand come from units with their own chains: one Matrix-12's video runs 5–7 dB brighter above 4 kHz than ob-poly-xp and two Xpanders, after the voice. And a Matrix-12 opens a filter envelope's short DELAY at once where an Xpander waits for it, as ob-poly-xp does.</dd></div>
</dl>
<div class="not">
<h3 class="h3">Not emulated</h3>
<p class="dim">The cassette interface; CV 1-6, GATE 1-6, TRIGGER, CHAIN ADVANCE and the pedal jacks, which read as unplugged (the unit's own MIDI CONTROLS page takes PEDAL 1 and 2 from MIDI controllers instead); temperature and drift; voice boards before revision D; factory multi patches (none has been found).</p>
</div>
<div id="help" class="help">
<div class="help-text">
<h3 class="h3">Own an Xpander or a Matrix-12?</h3>
<p>Recordings at known settings settle these: one held note at RES 62 and at 63 with the same FREQ; one low note in 4 POLE LOW at a series of RES values, from a VOICE output; a phaser mode at a known FREQ and RES.</p>
</div>
<a href="https://lemus.discourse.group" class="btn red">The forum</a>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="made" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<h2 class="h2 tap">How it was made</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li>
<div class="step-n">01</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">The firmware</h3>
<p class="dim">Both of the Xpander's processor boards run their original programs from reset on emulated 6809s (ported from MAME's core): the main board's (panel, displays, patches, modulation, MIDI) with its FIRQ timer and switch matrix, and the voice board's, which sets the 54 sample-and-holds through one 14-bit DAC, times the voices with its 8253 counter and runs TUNE. Every CV reaches the voices as the firmware writes it, cycle by cycle.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">02</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">Schematic and SPICE</h3>
<p class="dim">Each voice was transcribed from the service manual's schematic and modelled part by part: the two CEM3374 oscillators with their timing networks, the waveshapers and the pulse comparators, the mix buses and the FM paths, the CEM3372 filter with its 15 pole-mixing modes and resonance loop, the VCA, the pan section and the output stage, at four times the host's rate. Every block was compared with ngspice: 88 cases.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">03</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">The unit's own checks</h3>
<p class="dim">The service manual's checks run through the firmware and pass, TUNE ALL included: it measures each voice's oscillators, pulse widths, resonance, filter and VCA, and stores the corrections in battery RAM as on a real unit. A new copy of ob-poly-xp does what a new Xpander did: RESET MEMORY, then TUNE ALL.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">04</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">Real units</h3>
<p class="dim">Then recordings, per note and per patch: pitch against four sets of Xpander notes; the waveforms, the noise and the 15 filter modes against Matrix-12s (<a href={video('Yxxwb4d8GHA')}>Vulture Culture's</a>, <a href={video('NuBRcBlNAH8')}>gstormelectro's</a>); all 100 factory patches against <a href={video('NJrIT7_8N0Q')}>a factory demo</a> and a dry one (<a href={video('qfg9Jni8nN8')}>synthlegends'</a>), and a free user bank against a Matrix-12 playing it with every setting known. Where a unit and its recording differed from the others, ob-poly-xp follows what the units agree on (<a href="#differences">Differences</a>).</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>

<section id="rom" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec brief">
<h2 class="h2 tap">ROM images</h2>
<p class="dim">The firmware is in six 8 KB EPROMs, Oberheim's copyrighted work, so none is included: four main-board ROMs (software 1.2) and two voice-board ROMs (software 1.4), MAME's <span class="mono">xpander</span> set, with MAME's names. ob-poly-xp needs all six; without them it makes no sound. Oberheim's factory single patches, <span class="mono">FACTORY1.SYX</span>, are optional: with them a new unit starts on patch 00, without them the single patches start blank. <a href="/ob-poly-xp/guide#first-start">The ROMs</a></p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="artwork" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec brief last">
<h2 class="h2 tap">Panel artwork</h2>
<p class="dim">Oberheim's logo and the Xpander logo are trademarks, so their two places on the panel, and on the rear, are blank. Each place takes an SVG or PNG file of your own, named <span class="mono">ob-poly-xp-maker</span> and <span class="mono">ob-poly-xp-name</span>, in the ROM folder: drawn yourself, or generated with an AI tool such as Claude or ChatGPT. <a href="/ob-poly-xp/guide#artwork">Panel artwork</a></p>
</div>
</section>

</Base>

<script>
// Underline the strip's link for the section being read: the last whose top has passed under the
// strip, or the last of all at the foot of the page.
const links = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.onpage a')];
const targets = links.map((a) => document.getElementById(a.hash.slice(1))!);
const strip = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.onpage ol')!;
let current = -2;
function mark() {
  let i = -1;
  targets.forEach((t, j) => { if (t.getBoundingClientRect().top <= 80) i = j; });
  if (innerHeight + scrollY >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 2) i = targets.length - 1;
  if (i === current) return;
  current = i;
  links.forEach((a, j) => { if (j === i) a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location'); else a.removeAttribute('aria-current'); });
  // Bring the link into view along the strip only. scrollIntoView would also scroll the page, up
  // by the scroll padding, since the stuck strip sits inside it.
  const a = links[i];
  if (!a) return;
  const r = a.getBoundingClientRect(), s = strip.getBoundingClientRect();
  if (r.left < s.left) strip.scrollLeft += r.left - s.left;
  else if (r.right > s.right) strip.scrollLeft += r.right - s.right;
}
let frame = 0;
const later = () => { if (!frame) frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => { frame = 0; mark(); }); };
addEventListener('scroll', later, { passive: true });
addEventListener('resize', later);
mark();
</script>

